WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Ivana Korom
Reviewer: 

00:00:14.796 --> 00:00:16.556
[Baby cooing]

00:00:32.076 --> 00:00:34.796
What if I was to tell you

00:00:34.836 --> 00:00:39.756
that a game of peekaboo
could change the world?

00:00:41.076 --> 00:00:43.356
Sounds impossible, right?

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:47.756
Well, I’m here today to prove it’s not.

00:00:48.436 --> 00:00:50.116
Hi, I’m Molly and I’m seven.

00:00:50.156 --> 00:00:52.436
And this is my little friend, Ari.

00:00:52.636 --> 00:00:54.316
Say “Hi,” Ari.

00:00:55.276 --> 00:00:56.596
Hi.

00:00:57.116 --> 00:01:00.156
Oh, and this is my neighbor, Amarjot.

00:01:00.756 --> 00:01:05.036
He has to take Ari away now
to get ready for our experiment.

00:01:05.156 --> 00:01:07.516
But don't worry, they'll be back.

00:01:08.916 --> 00:01:12.516
My talk today is about some powerful
things you grownups can do.

00:01:12.556 --> 00:01:15.756
that shape us as children
and the adults we become.

00:01:16.556 --> 00:01:18.116
How do I know?

00:01:18.156 --> 00:01:22.316
Because my parents and people around me
did them early and often.

00:01:23.156 --> 00:01:25.396
I know not all kids are as lucky.

00:01:25.436 --> 00:01:29.716
Some of my friends, some kids at my school
and many around the world.

00:01:30.196 --> 00:01:32.916
And I would really like
to help change that.

00:01:34.116 --> 00:01:35.876
Thanks to scientists,

00:01:35.876 --> 00:01:39.236
we now know just how important
the first five years are

00:01:39.276 --> 00:01:41.356
for our health and development,

00:01:41.396 --> 00:01:43.236
especially our brains.

00:01:43.276 --> 00:01:46.396
Ari started learning
long before he was even born,

00:01:46.436 --> 00:01:48.316
from inside his mommy’s tummy.

00:01:48.716 --> 00:01:50.436
When Ari was born,

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:55.236
he was tiny and he cried all the time.

00:01:55.356 --> 00:01:59.596
He was always hungry
and he pooped a lot.

00:01:59.636 --> 00:02:01.436
[Laughter]

00:02:01.476 --> 00:02:05.236
Now he laughs and giggles
and makes funny noises.

00:02:05.276 --> 00:02:08.596
But those are just the changes
we hear and see.

00:02:08.636 --> 00:02:11.596
That’s way more going on inside.

00:02:12.316 --> 00:02:16.196
So, let’s talk more about our brains.

00:02:16.556 --> 00:02:22.076
The blue bag is a rough size and weight
of a healthy baby’s brain at birth.

00:02:22.356 --> 00:02:26.876
The red one is a baby’s brain
after the first year.

00:02:26.916 --> 00:02:29.436
It almost doubles in volume.

00:02:30.036 --> 00:02:35.836
And by my age, it's almost 90 percent
the volume of an adult brain.

00:02:38.396 --> 00:02:41.036
Our brains develop faster
in our early years

00:02:41.276 --> 00:02:44.756
than at any other time 
in our lives.

00:02:45.636 --> 00:02:50.676
It can create up to one million
neural connections every second.

00:02:51.476 --> 00:02:53.036
But we need your help.

00:02:53.996 --> 00:02:58.396
Our healthy development depends
on these top five things,

00:02:58.516 --> 00:03:01.996
One, connecting; two, talking;

00:03:02.036 --> 00:03:05.876
three, playing; four, a healthy home,

00:03:05.916 --> 00:03:07.836
five; community.

00:03:07.996 --> 00:03:12.236
All of this helps our brains
and us reach our full potential.

00:03:12.516 --> 00:03:16.956
So what’s something you can do
that can really make a difference?

00:03:17.356 --> 00:03:20.316
Scientists call it serve and return.

00:03:20.676 --> 00:03:26.356
That’s just a grown-up way of saying
connect, talk and play with us.

00:03:26.596 --> 00:03:29.836
And here’s the really big news.

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:31.956
Amarjot, Ari, you ready?

00:03:31.956 --> 00:03:33.436
(Audio) Amajat: Ready?

00:03:33.476 --> 00:03:37.036
Molly Wright: Copycat games
build imagination and empathy.

00:03:37.516 --> 00:03:41.436
(Amarjot and Ari laughing)

00:03:41.716 --> 00:03:45.756
Naming games build vocabulary
and attention.

00:03:45.796 --> 00:03:50.396
Amarjot: Daddy. Ari. Daddy. Ari.

00:03:50.436 --> 00:03:53.276
MW: And games like peekabo --
yep, peekaboo --

00:03:53.316 --> 00:03:55.596
actually build memory and trust.

00:03:56.156 --> 00:03:58.556
Amarjot: Peekaboo!

00:03:58.596 --> 00:04:03.196
MW: Each time you talk to us,
play with us, make us laugh,

00:04:03.236 --> 00:04:08.556
it not only builds and strengthens
our relationships and mental health,

00:04:08.596 --> 00:04:12.756
it actually teaches us
some of the most important life skills,

00:04:12.796 --> 00:04:15.716
from making friends to taking the test,

00:04:15.756 --> 00:04:16.916
to getting a job,

00:04:16.956 --> 00:04:20.876
to one day maybe even starting
a family of our own.

00:04:21.436 --> 00:04:24.276
Interactions early and often matter.

00:04:24.316 --> 00:04:25.476
Take it from me,

00:04:25.516 --> 00:04:29.036
the seven-year-old up here
talking about brain science.

00:04:29.076 --> 00:04:30.516
(Laughter)

00:04:30.556 --> 00:04:34.916
OK, now let’s see what happens
when the connection is taken away.

00:04:37.756 --> 00:04:40.676
So now he’s trying to get
his dad’s attention again.

00:04:40.716 --> 00:04:44.396
He’s reaching out like,
“That was fun, why have you stopped?”

00:04:46.596 --> 00:04:48.556
(Ari cries)

00:04:48.596 --> 00:04:53.436
I know it’s important for adults
to use their devices sometimes,

00:04:53.476 --> 00:04:58.316
but kids are hardwired
to seek out meaningful connections,

00:04:58.356 --> 00:05:02.076
not receiving them
causes confusion and stress.

00:05:02.956 --> 00:05:06.076
OK, Amarjot, please re-engage.

00:05:06.556 --> 00:05:08.476
Amarjot: Ah, there’s Ari.

00:05:08.676 --> 00:05:11.236
(Ari laughs)

00:05:13.436 --> 00:05:19.356
MW: Now what if our whole childhood
was like that last 30 seconds?

00:05:19.916 --> 00:05:22.636
How hard it would be
for a child to feel calm.

00:05:23.676 --> 00:05:25.516
To feel safe.

00:05:26.156 --> 00:05:28.516
To learn to trust anyone.

00:05:28.556 --> 00:05:31.556
And the lifelong impact that would have.

00:05:32.676 --> 00:05:34.796
That makes me feel sad.

00:05:38.796 --> 00:05:43.756
Ari only reacted the way he did
and recovered so quickly

00:05:43.796 --> 00:05:47.996
because the connection between him
and his dad is usually so strong.

00:05:48.756 --> 00:05:51.836
The positive relationships
with the grown-ups in our lives

00:05:51.876 --> 00:05:54.756
gives kids the confidence we need

00:05:54.796 --> 00:06:00.796
to try new things, to explore 
and be a kid.

00:06:01.916 --> 00:06:04.396
So please,

00:06:04.436 --> 00:06:09.116
try to remember the most special period
for our development is

00:06:09.156 --> 00:06:12.236
the first five years.

00:06:12.396 --> 00:06:15.196
Starting from inside mommy’s tummy.

00:06:15.276 --> 00:06:18.116
What’s something really
impactful you can do?

00:06:18.556 --> 00:06:21.116
Serve and return.

00:06:21.916 --> 00:06:23.636
And when?

00:06:23.756 --> 00:06:26.036
Early and often.

00:06:26.876 --> 00:06:29.756
Please give it up for a Amarjot and Ari.

00:06:29.836 --> 00:06:36.796
(Applause)

00:06:37.516 --> 00:06:41.516
Every moment together is an opportunity

00:06:41.556 --> 00:06:44.676
to connect, talk and play.

00:06:45.476 --> 00:06:51.836
Imagine the difference we could make
if everyone everywhere did this.

00:06:53.356 --> 00:06:56.756
To us, the children,

00:06:56.796 --> 00:07:01.116
it’s so much more than just a game.

00:07:04.156 --> 00:07:05.556
It's our future.

00:07:06.076 --> 00:07:07.076
Thank you.

00:07:07.116 --> 00:07:08.396
(Applause)

00:07:08.436 --> 00:07:11.676
See? Peekaboo really can change the world.

00:07:11.716 --> 00:07:13.636
(Applause)

